00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:15.980
Imagine one decision you've made recently.

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:22.220
We all make hundreds of decisions every day. Some of them much more important than others. Take for example your morning commute.

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:28.399
Every day you go from point A, your home, to point B, your place of employment. On the way,

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:35.390
you'll make numerous decisions: Should I go the speed limit or just a little over or a lot over? Should I stop for coffee:

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:39.410
Starbucks or Dunkin donuts? Should I clip this guy on the bike

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:41.160
who's clearly not in the bike lane?

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:46.399
You get the picture. In each of these scenarios, you make your decision and move on, and any other

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:53.569
possibilities become null and void. But what if you really performed all of those possible actions simultaneously. It sounds impossible,

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:58.100
but the general concept is a staple and what's broadly referred to as "Multiverse Theory."

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:01.669
The idea is that for every potential outcome for any given situation,

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:05.839
There is a separate and distinct universe in which that outcome did happen.

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:09.589
So there would be a separate universe for stopping at Starbucks on your way to work, and

00:01:09.659 --> 00:01:12.589
another one for stopping at Dunkin Donuts, each occurring simultaneously

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:17.449
in their own unique timeline. If we extrapolate this to existence in general,

00:01:17.450 --> 00:01:23.689
there's an alternate timeline in which your favorite sports team didn't lose that big game, one where the Russians beat the United States to the

00:01:23.689 --> 00:01:28.789
moon, one where Henry Ford decided to collect stamps instead of pursue his idea of a motorized carriage.

00:01:29.549 --> 00:01:33.979
Basically, anything that could happen does happen and things that couldn't happen

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:36.589
also happen, but we're getting ahead of ourselves.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:42.110
The idea that there could be more universes in existence than just our own dates back to at least

00:01:42.659 --> 00:01:49.909
1952 when Aaron Schrodinger stated that when his nobel equations seemed to describe several different histories these were not alternatives

00:01:49.909 --> 00:01:51.909
But all really happened simultaneously

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:59.239
that being said it's nearly impossible to pin down a date for the inception of multiverse theory because it consists of many very different and

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:06.859
Often fundamentally opposing hypotheses for example the idea of parallel or alternate timelines exists not only in physics and astronomy

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:12.919
But also cosmology religion philosophy and psychology not to mention science fiction and Fantasy literature

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:17.522
But let's look at the scientific side of things what kind of support is there

00:02:17.522 --> 00:02:19.759
for the theory of multiple universes?

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:25.880
Not much there has been and continues to be heated debate over whether or not multiverse theory holds water

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:31.429
But more than that many scientists believe that not only is the whole idea silly it could erode public

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:38.749
perception of real science and damage the study of fundamental physics Harsh accusations to be sure but not without cause

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:41.810
The basis of the Scientific method is asking a question

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:45.619
forming a testable hypothesis, gathering empirical data and

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:52.519
Evaluating the findings. the scientists opposed to the study of multiverse theory suggests that this testability is the key issue

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:57.620
How can any experiment rule out a theory if the theory provides for all possible outcomes?

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:05.690
This is a valid Concern but for the sake of this video let's explore a few different possible types of multiverse the American theoretical

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:11.000
physicist and string theorist Brian Greene lists nine possible variants of multiverse each with unique

00:03:11.579 --> 00:03:15.949
Characteristics, we'll take a look at four of the more interesting ones first

00:03:15.950 --> 00:03:21.229
There's the possibility of what's called a quilted universe this explanation only works in an infinite universe

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:24.260
But if we assume that our own universe is infinite

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:25.769
It's not an unreasonable

00:03:25.769 --> 00:03:29.689
Hypothesis, in a quilted universe since distance and time is infinite

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:34.880
Every possible event within our laws of physics will happen an infinite number of times. however

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:40.250
the speed of light would prevent us from ever seeing the evidence of such identical sections of universe. that

00:03:40.829 --> 00:03:44.149
seems fairly straightforward right? let's look at a slightly more complicated

00:03:44.459 --> 00:03:51.888
variant. the Membrane or Brane hypothesis suggests that many separate universes exist in three-dimensional form within a larger

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:56.750
Multi-dimensional space or bulk think of it this way when you buy printer paper

00:03:56.750 --> 00:04:04.069
You get hundreds of sheets bundled inside a wrapper the wrapper is like the bulk and the individual sheets of paper are unique universes

00:04:04.319 --> 00:04:11.238
within this bulk the universes can drift around and interact with one another and every few trillion years or so drawn together by

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:16.849
gravity or other forces we can't yet comprehend. They collide with one another in a violent explosion triggering

00:04:16.849 --> 00:04:18.720
what we know as a big bang. at

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:23.510
that point they could bounce off each other like billiard balls combined into a new super universe or

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:26.629
Perhaps most likely given our current understanding of the Big bang

00:04:26.849 --> 00:04:30.379
spark the creation of a new universe from the violent destruction of the old ones

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:35.840
Then there's the theory most reminiscent of the example at the beginning of this video. The quantum universe

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:41.330
hypothesis. this version of the multiverse theory suggests that a new you is created every time a

00:04:41.490 --> 00:04:44.479
Diversion event occurs such as choosing to call in sick to work

00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:46.759
there would be one universe based on the

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:52.189
Consequences of going into work like normal and another new one for the events that transpire after calling in sick

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:59.360
this multiverse would grow exponentially and infinitely as every person on Earth makes hundreds of decisions every [day] and

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:01.759
Those decisions cause a new set of decisions

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:08.300
And so on it's a fascinating theory and one that seems to capture public interest more than others finally

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:13.909
There's the ultimate multiverse hypothesis this one tends to draw a lot of criticism from the scientific community

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:15.530
Perhaps Rightfully

00:05:15.530 --> 00:05:22.579
So the ultimate multiverse hypothesis suggests that it would contain every mathematically possible universe under different laws of physics

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:25.460
[that] last part is what gets it in trouble

00:05:25.530 --> 00:05:29.899
Not only does it consider our laws of physics the ones we assume to be universal

00:05:29.900 --> 00:05:36.019
But [it] goes so far as to say all possible universes within all possible variants of physics. There's simply

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:39.770
No way to test the hypothesis which relegates it more to the realm of philosophy

00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:46.039
Than hard science of course many scientists consider the multiverse theory as a whole to [be] more philosophical than empirical

00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:49.819
Whether they're right, or not remains to be seen as so far

00:05:49.820 --> 00:05:54.170
We don't have any evidence for the existence of any universe, but our own, but hey

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:59.659
Maybe we're in a universe where the laws of physics don't allow us to detect the billions of other universes floating all around us

